{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: two P2 scheduling/failure defects at exact head 611edea767fcde542ee4dc1b4b8ec2cdaa86091f. Complete plan 003 and all six delivered paths reviewed against approved main 93a2552e45c5a46c4dcf624e28c7c703d987e114. The original finding 1473992512c5bf7f973bfb8044cb45edebd7b7c5 is repaired: pending terminal cleanup no longer blocks later preparation, reservation or publication. Independent pending-push and 25-token controls pass. Estimates have not reduced scope.\n\nP2 — Schedule pending cleanup without repeatedly requesting immediate Room alarms. engine.ts:350-356 starts a background pass but nextDue() still returns the cleanup record's original due time. Until revoke answers or times out, that due time remains in the past. The production Room's alarm returns from reconcile, calls schedule, and alarmTime clamps the overdue time to Date.now()+10 ms. Further alarms perform the whole Room work cycle while startCleanup skips the already-running pass, then request another immediate wake. A pass can wait 30 seconds per token and processes up to 20 tokens sequentially; remaining due records also keep the minimum in the past. Single flight bounds provider dispatch, but does not bound these unnecessary Room wake-ups.\n\nIndependent real workerd control uses the unchanged production Room, actual Landing and Durable Object SQLite, a successfully landed operation with known cleanup debt, real Date.now scheduling and one held provider revocation. Five real Room.alarm runs each store the next alarm 9–10 ms away; provider call count remains one, debt remains unchanged and overdue. The named control 'checker: pending terminal cleanup does not continually schedule an already due alarm' fails. A diagnostic-only nextDue deferral for an otherwise idle pending pass makes the same control pass; restored exact source fails again. This demonstrates repeated immediate alarm requests, not a measured Cloudflare delivery-rate or timing SLA. Fixture: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-diagnostics/packages/room/test/workerd/checker-terminal-alarm.test.ts. Logs: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-alarm-exact-final.log and /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-alarm-defer-control.log. The report's claim of one extra wake about a second later applies to the fast-answer stand-in test, whose alarm floor is one second; it does not cover this pending case.\n\nRepair the cleanup scheduling contract so a pass in progress has a bounded future wake/recovery time, and other due work can still determine an earlier alarm. Preserve later publication progress, single flight, eventual service of all owed records, timeout/backoff, restart ownership, stopped-instance fencing and held-slot recovery priority. Add a production Room alarm control for pending/slow batches, completion and timeout, and update the report. Do not put cleanup back on the publication chain or hide debt indefinitely.\n\nP2 — Retry failed cleanup completion with backoff. engine.ts:375-382 catches failures only from revokeWithin; tokenRevoked is now outside that handler. If Artifacts answers but the atomic completion transaction throws, the outer pass catch silently ends the batch. The rolled-back debt keeps its old past due time and 1000 ms backoff, so the next alarm immediately repeats revocation and completion instead of taking the durable failure backoff. The previous engine caught completion failures as well as provider failures.\n\nIndependent real DO SQLite control inserts an actual BEFORE DELETE trigger on artroom_land_token_cleanup after publication has landed. The provider revocation succeeds, completion rolls back, durable tokenRevoked remains false, the receipt remains unique and the slot stays free. Expected future backoff 2000 ms is still 1000 ms and due is unchanged: named test 'checker: terminal cleanup insertion and completion faults roll back atomically across real DO restarts' fails directly on that assertion. Extending the diagnostic handler around completion, while retaining the stopped-instance check, makes the whole control pass, including trigger removal and restart cleanup; restored source fails again. Fixture: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-diagnostics/packages/git/test-workers/checker-terminal-sql.test.ts. Logs: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-sql-exact-final.log and /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-sql-catch-control.log. These are transactional SQL statement errors, not a claim that a failed underlying Cloudflare storage write bypasses output gates.\n\nRetain debt and atomic completion on this failure path, but give it the same durable capped retry policy as other cleanup failures whenever storage can record that retry. Keep late/dead answers from writing, preserve the other records in a bounded batch, and add this failure-path regression. Both repairs stay on the same implementation request/promise; no change of outcome, conditions, authority, performer or target is requested.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are the Git landing state machine/driver and Room Durable Object scheduler, within application execution over storage in the gitseq architecture model. Artroom has no docs/reference/architecture.md in this tree; docs/protocol.md remains its public contract. No Gitseq kernel or Workroom fold change, public LandOp/receipt migration, policy/admission authority change, CAS/slot ordering change or publication-outcome change is introduced. Private per-token ownership and background cleanup are appropriate; the two defects violate plan 003's independent, bounded scheduling and durable future retry requirements.\n\nSecurity: checked recorded token attribution, provider refusal/timeout, late answers, restart adoption, SQL atomicity, batch bounds, expiry and public secret-free views. Known token IDs alone drive cleanup; no shared canonical inventory sweep or unrelated revocation is introduced. No expiry or revoke answer is treated as proof of a publication outcome. The unresolved resource/failure scheduling defects prevent approval. Canonical unknown-mint ownership and cleanup visibility remain separate tracked work.\n\nSimplification: retain the existing private duty table, one-time legacy adoption, bounded selection and capped backoff. Complete the small scheduling/failure contract above, without adding another outcome machine or weakening functional scope. The removed settle wait is unnecessary; the equivalent N6 mutation supplies no evidence and is not counted.\n\nFresh validation at the exact clean head in /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2: root npm run typecheck exit 0; root npm test exit 0, 1981 passed and one skipped; Git Worker suite 9 passed; Git log suite 12 passed; targeted landing 50 passed. Dependencies were copied from a previously validated installed checkout with identical package.json/lockfile; this is not an independent npm ci. Scope/whitespace checks pass: 602 tracked paths, 596 byte-identical to approved main, five scoped existing paths modified and the assigned plan added. The candidate checkout remained clean.\n\nIn a separate diagnostic clone, all 26 non-equivalent guards (M1–M20, N1–N5 and M7b) independently produced named runtime failures without compile/import failures, then production sources were restored. Evidence: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v2-mutants.json and individual mutation logs. Independent pending-publication and 20-then-five batch controls pass after restoration. The two newly identified controls remain red on exact source and pass only under their respective diagnostic changes. No production change, deployment, live credential, inference or remote resource operation was made. The separately promised follow-up at 371f1738 addresses snapshot wake, founded-room recovery and jobs inventory; it does not repair these landing-driver defects and is reviewed separately.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5ad837bfd912086325a9707385a89e71c62ece71","binding":"assigned","head